  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="tool-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="split-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">The studio</p>
          <h2 id="tool-h">Make codes, write rules, or check lock-in</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="muted">Nine QR types, own-domain forwarding rules for seven hosts, a copy-paste snippet for site builders, and a local photo check to plan your move off vendor codes. Everything stays on your device.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="qr-app" id="generator" data-qr-app>
        <noscript>
          <div class="card qr-noscript">
            <h3>JavaScript is needed to run Bandit Codes locally</h3>
            <p>Bandit Codes draws, scan-checks, and decodes QR codes inside your browser tab so nothing is ever uploaded to a server. Enable JavaScript for this page to use the tool.</p>
          </div>
        </noscript>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band alt" aria-labelledby="why-break-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="split-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">The plain truth</p>
          <h2 id="why-break-h">Why printed QR codes break</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="muted">A subscription "dynamic" QR code prints a short link on a vendor's domain. When the plan ends or the service closes, that domain stops forwarding and printed pieces stop working. Bandit Codes cannot bring back a code that already points at a vendor; it helps you stop making them and plan the replacement at your own pace, high-use pieces first.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="grid g2 sub sub3">
        <li class="card">
          <p class="tag">The lock-in trap</p>
          <h3>Vendor short links</h3>
          <p>The code points at another company's domain. Even major free shorteners like <code>goo.gl</code> and <code>git.io</code> stopped making new links. Printed codes on a vendor's domain stop working when the vendor stops forwarding, no matter what tool you switch to.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <p class="tag">Your own address</p>
          <h3>Your own domain</h3>
          <p>Print a short path on your own website, like <code>yoursite.com/go/menu</code>. Add one forwarding line on your host (or paste the Bandit Codes snippet on a site builder) and visitors land straight on the destination with no extra page in between. This works as long as you keep your domain and website. <a href="/tools/qr-code-generator/guides/">Read the step-by-step install guides for 11 website platforms</a>.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <p class="tag">Changing files and bulk lists</p>
          <h3>Files others update</h3>
          <p>Your code points at a PDF someone else updates, like pamphlet A becoming A.1? Print your own web address on the code. It forwards straight to the current file with no lookup page, and editing one line updates every printed copy. Have a long list of codes? Import a spreadsheet (CSV) or paste two columns to build forwarding rules or a snippet for all of them at once.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <p class="tag">No domain needed</p>
          <h3>Direct static codes</h3>
          <p>Put the final web address, Wi-Fi login, or contact card straight into the pattern. It needs no host setup and stays valid as long as the destination page exists. The trade-off is plain: to change a link later, you reprint that code.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="how-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="split-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Under the hood</p>
          <h2 id="how-h">Private by design, portable by default</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="muted">Nothing leaves your device. Optional browser saving stays off until you turn it on, and every list exports cleanly to CSV and JSON.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="grid g3 sub sub3">
        <li class="card">
          <p class="num">01</p>
          <h3>Runs on your device</h3>
          <p>New codes, uploaded photos of printed codes, forwarding rules, snippets, and escape plans stay inside this tab. No account, no upload, and no tracking.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <p class="num">02</p>
          <h3>Checked before you print</h3>
          <p>Every new code is drawn in your browser and read back at multiple sizes the way a phone camera would, alongside a rule-of-thumb print width.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <p class="num">03</p>
          <h3>MIT open source</h3>
          <p>Take your link lists anywhere in CSV or JSON, and read or fork the full <a href={GITHUB_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">source code on GitHub</a>.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted small" style="margin-top:1.5rem">QR Code is a registered trademark of DENSO WAVE INCORPORATED.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

